There can be many ways to solve this problem. Here, we will do this by thinking about *units*.

Let's say Trevor's family paid for water at a rate of $x\,\dfrac{\text{dollars}}{\text{minute}}$. We are given that the price of water is $0.20\,\dfrac{\text{dollars}}{\text{liter}}$. How can we relate these two quantities with an equation?

$\begin{align}
y\,\dfrac{\text{liters}}{\text{minute}}\cdot 0.2\,\dfrac{\text{dollars}}{\text{liter}}=x\,\dfrac{\text{dollars}}{\text{minute}}
\end{align}$

So in order to find  $x$, the cost per minute, we need to figure out the value of $y$, which is the rate of liters used per minute of shower.

We can combine the following quantities:

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{72\,\dfrac{\text{liters}}{\text{person}}}{480\,\dfrac{\text{minutes}}{\text{person}}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{72}{480}\,\dfrac{\text{liters}}{\cancel{\text{person}}}\cdot\dfrac{\cancel{\text{persons}}}{\text{minute}}
\\\\
&=0.15\,\dfrac{\text{liters}}{\text{minute}}
\end{align}$

Now we can plug that in the original equation:

$\begin{align}
0.15\,\dfrac{\text{liter}}{\text{minute}}\cdot 0.2\,\dfrac{\text{dollars}}{\text{liter}}&=x\,\dfrac{\text{dollars}}{\text{minute}}
\\\\
0.03\,\dfrac{\text{dollars}}{\text{minute}}&=x\,\dfrac{\text{dollars}}{\text{minute}}
\end{align}$
